### 2026-11-03 — Batch 19: where the chain's sections lie, and what it reports of one it reads in part ([#156], [#188], [#197], [#207], [#208], [#210], [#221], [#222], [#241], [#261], [#264], [#281], [#317])
- **The question.** Measured on `main` at `21a0298` by nine agents, one for each part of the batch, and a completeness critic who composed their changes and looked for what none of them could see. [#188] and [#281]: a section the chain names that is there and cannot be read was looked for nearby as one that is not there, and landed on a section the chain had read — a newer table, the table that named it, a hybrid table's own stream —, reported as a loop and a shift the file did not have, `Error xref.chain-loop` among them, and, where a hybrid table was taken for the stream its `/XRefStm` names, an object read as null with nothing said; of the 735 sections the chains of the 403 corpus documents name, one was relocated, in IBM's QMF manual, onto the right table. [#197]: near the file's start the search near an offset reached 1,024 bytes past it, not 512, and a window that began inside a header's number served another object's body. [#261]: neither the search near a row nor the rebuild's scan read a header with a comment between its tokens, which a load reads, and digits a comment ended with were taken for a number. [#208]: the chain stopped at a cross-reference stream whose rows could not be read — a width negative or no number, an `/Index` or a `/Size` that numbers nothing, a deferred `/Length` no `endstream` confirms — though its dictionary named the older sections, and the file was rebuilt; the issue's own shape, a width past 8, had been paid. [#207], [#210] and [#222]: a table the end of the file cuts before its trailer, a section that lost rows to a row that is no row or a stream short of its rows, and a table that holds no subsection opened with no report, an object the cut took lost in silence or found by a rebuild whose `xref.rebuilt` was all that was said, a false `file.size-wrong` beside them. [#156]: an `/XRefStm` naming a section already read, or a classic table, left no record and no finding, and a stream two tables name counted for one, so that a PDFMaker 7 document of the OPF corpus drew a false `file.size-wrong`. [#221]: a row of a reserved type was skipped, an older section's row standing where ISO 32000-1 reads null. [#264]: a section a rows guard stopped judged nothing of what it names, a `/Prev` naming a free row, an offset outside the file or the section itself reported as the guard alone. [#241]: 1,072 lines of the reports of 28 corpus documents put a count of one in the plural, or a distance back as a negative count. [#317]: under four busy loops the row the issue names went past its bound of 8 in one run of ten, and the row of members written as streams in five, up to 10.96 — a collection inside the large probe, which kept every finding, 15.6 MB for 50,000 members and 63.1 MB for 200,000. Twelve questions followed.
- **Settled with the maintainer**, eleven of the twelve as recommended (2026-11-03):
  - a section is there where its offset holds the `xref` keyword or a stream whose dictionary gives `/Type /XRef`: one that cannot be read is reported where it is, never looked for nearby, and a stream of `/Type /XRef` hands its `/Prev` on ([#208]); an object that gives only a `/W` stays a guess, looked for nearby, as [#263]'s tests have it;
  - no older section's row stands for a number that a section which lost rows declares — a stream that cannot be read and hands its `/Prev` on, a stream read in part, a hybrid table's stream that cannot be read —, the ranges kept to 4,096, an internal constant (ADR 34), past which every number is refused, with a timing test of 1,024 chained streams that cannot be read;
  - a section read only in part is reported under `xref.section-unreadable`, its message saying that it was read only in part and why, the code's summary widened to a section that cannot be read whole, `startxref`'s included, the validator unchanged; a first section that cannot be read at all, which leaves only `xref.rebuilt`, filed ([#323]);
  - a table that holds no subsection reads as indexing nothing, as `0 0` does, its index whole, the reader reporting `xref.section-empty`, a repair, and a new rule `xref.subsection-missing`, a warning ([#222]);
  - an `/XRefStm` that names no cross-reference stream of its table's revision is reported by the reader as `xref.hybrid-stream-lost`, a warning, the index left incomplete, and by a new rule, `xref.hybrid-stream-wrong`, an error; `xref.chain-cycle` and `xref.chain-loop` keep to `/Prev`: an `/XRefStm` naming what its table's `/Prev` names is not followed, that `/Prev` reading it whole ([#156]);
  - a cross-reference stream row of a reserved type reads as the null object, indexed as a free entry is, no older row standing for it, the reader reporting `xref.entry-reserved` and a new rule `xref.entry-type-reserved`, both warnings, which say that ISO 32000-1 defines types 0, 1 and 2 ([#221]);
  - [#317]'s probe given a validation's default room for findings, 1,000, with five turns and its bound of 8, in the test alone: a fourth way of steadying a timing test, which the maintainer consented to;
  - one notion of what the chain has read — the positions where it read or relocated a section, a header's offset counted — for its loop check, an `/XRefStm` naming a stream again ([#156]), what a guard's stop leaves judged ([#264]) and relocation;
  - `trailer.root-recovered` reported once a document;
  - a hybrid table that copies an older revision's `/XRefStm` two revisions or more late filed under M02, with how to detect it, its rule left to M03's writer ([#321]); and the rows a relocation candidate leaves when it is dropped filed under M02 with both designs, a mark to roll back to or rows held until the candidate is taken ([#320]).

  Against the recommendation, which had left it to a debt: the rule a damaged stream's `endstream` search stops at follows the rebuild's scan in this batch, taking a header with comments between its tokens, a comment carried across its reads, so that such a stream stops at that header, two rows of `StreamLengthTests` turning; the texts that said the rule refuses such a header, the record of 2026-09-30 below among them, say it follows the scan.

  Decided on the measures, without a question: [#197]'s window, 512 bytes either side of the offset wherever it falls, one that does not start the file reading one byte before its start, a header or a section counting only where it starts inside it — [#171]'s left edge, paid for the search near a row; [#261]'s header confirmed forward from a token boundary, its line lexed by the load's lexer, the tests that hid a header from the scan with a comment hiding it with a signed generation, through one helper ([#327]); [#207]'s cut table read in part where it read a row, unreadable where it read none, and `file.size-wrong` judging no chain that holds a section read in part; [#210]'s report made once, at the section, for its first fault, a section a guard cut that lost no row left the guard's, and the section `startxref` names read in part not rebuilt at opening; [#208]'s handed `/Prev` followed under the chain's positions, its count guard and relocation, the stream's dictionary never taken for the trailer, `file.trailer-value-wrong` judging that `/Prev` alone; [#156]'s classic table read in the stream's place reported, the index left incomplete, and a stream two tables name read once, counted for each and its `/Size` judged where the oldest names it; [#264]'s guarded `/Prev` and `/XRefStm` judged as a whole read judges them; [#221] reported by the reader and the validator alike; [#241]'s distances said "before" or "after", its counts agreeing with their noun through one helper the reader and the validator share, each site keeping its digit grouping ([#326]); and the sixteen spacers the tests had put between sections, to keep them out of the search near an offset, removed.

  The reviews put twenty more questions to the maintainer, each answered as recommended:
  - 2026-11-04, after the first round: a relocation candidate that declares itself a section and cannot be read ends the search where it is the nearest, taken for the section found moved — `xref.offset-adjusted`, then `xref.section-unreadable` there, its declared numbers refused, a stream of `/Type /XRef` read whole handing its `/Prev` on, the validator giving `xref.section-shifted` and `xref.section-malformed` —, which changes what [#320] expects; a classic table an `/XRefStm` names that cannot be read refuses what it surely lost, and every number where what follows its fault cannot be known; the mirror order of [#156], an `/XRefStm` read first and a later `/Prev` reaching its stream reported as a loop, filed under M02 ([#330]), `file.size-wrong` judging no chain that ends in a loop; and [#317]'s test timing four probes back to back each turn, held over some 300 runs under four busy loops.
  - 2026-11-06, after the fourth: a section a link names is read from the first token past its white space, the white space and the dictionary each bounded by `MaxTrailerLength`, white space past it reaching `limit.trailer` there.
  - 2026-11-07, after the sixth: the search near an offset taking `xref`, or a header of `/Type /XRef`, in uncompressed stream data for a section found moved — worse than `main` since the first round's decision — documented and pinned by tests under [#171], with no change of code.
  - 2026-11-07, after the seventh: a cross-reference stream whose filters decode nothing of its data — a filter the library does not know, Flate data corrupt before its first byte — holds no rows, its fault that its data could not be decoded, its numbers refused and its `/Prev` handed on, the filter pipeline saying so by a value of its own and encoded bytes no longer read as rows; a stream near a hybrid table's `/Prev` that may be the table's own told by its own `/Prev`, one that gives none, or the table's, tried after every other place; and a section in stream data that reads, taken before any line is lexed, left to [#171].
  - 2026-11-07, after the eighth, on a measurement of the rules proposed over 3,210 hybrid files generated for it, read under six sets of limits, 9,228 readings: a stream near the table's `/Prev` may be the table's own only where it starts at or after the stream the table's `/XRefStm` read or names again, one before it keeping its nearest-first place, with what that costs accepted — one shape back to `main`'s rebuild and its false `file.root-invalid`, two generated files that read a first revision's stream some 500 bytes off, the revisions between lost with only a section found moved reported ([#333]), and a test whose file no appending writer makes rewritten —; its `/Prev` compared as the chain reads it, a real with no fraction as its integer, a reference as what a section read places there, an offset outside the file as another offset; and a stream an `/XRefStm` names again standing for the one it read, never taken in its place, which is filed ([#332]). The rule chosen read 1,193 of the readings worse than `main` by the measurement's classing, a chain read whole where `main` rebuilt counting among them, and 69 better, against 1,434 and 69 under the rule it replaced; no later round moved those counts. Told to the maintainer before those questions, as invariant 5 settles it: a stream the search passes over as possibly the table's own, which the chain never reads, is reported, `xref.stream-passed-over`, a warning.
  - 2026-11-08, after the ninth: a place before the stream an `/XRefStm` read whose header's number runs into where that reading started is that stream; and `xref.stream-passed-over` names only a place that declares itself — `/Type /XRef`, its header a token on its line —, nothing read for it. That day the maintainer let builds and test runs share the machine, benchmarks and the timing, budget, allocation and memory tests apart, and the batch moved to the maintainer's Windows machine.
  - 2026-11-08, after the eleventh: once white space past `MaxTrailerLength` meets the chain where it names a section, the warning is withheld throughout the document, even where the search finds the section whole past the run, which only a bound under the search's 512 bytes allows; [#289]'s closing may bring it back.
  - 2026-11-10, after the twelfth: the flag that withholds the warning set wherever a trailer guard cut the reading at an offset the chain names, whatever the cut left — a dictionary of any `/Type`, a header, a comment —; and the stream an `/XRefStm` read, and every stream section the chain reached from a sign of its header, told by where the header's digits start, whatever the bytes searched hold, so that no such header is read twice in a loop.
  - 2026-11-10, before the thirteenth: a header found at its first sign, a `%` on the line of its number, told by the digits past its signs, as it is without the `%`.
- **Reviewed** thirteen times, each finding put to a second agent that tried to refute it, most of them in a worktree of their own, 328 findings in all, 14 of them refuted. On 2026-11-10 the maintainer amended the rule the loop stops by, from the thirteenth round on: it ends at the first round in which no blocker and no major survives refutation, rather than no minor, that round's minors and nits getting one final pass and no review after it — a minor that needs a change of behavior filed or put to the maintainer —, the fourteenth round at the latest, a blocker or a major left after it going to the maintainer. The thirteenth met it. Every blocker and major was fixed in the batch, but the search's reading of stream data, documented and pinned under [#171] at the maintainer's choice, and what the rules of 2026-11-07 accepted, filed as [#332] and [#333].
  - The first round, on the implementation, 49 findings, ten major: the chain told a section it had reached by the offset written rather than by where the section's first token starts, so that a link naming the white space before a section read it again and doubled its reports, and an `/XRefStm` reaching what its table's `/Prev` reads through another offset went unseen; a relocation passed over the nearest place that declares itself a section and cannot be read; a guarded `/Prev` naming a number a newer section refused went unjudged; a table a guard cut before its first row was decided by what the index held, and the end of the file inside the `trailer` keyword read as a stray token; a classic table an `/XRefStm` names that cannot be read let older rows stand for what it lost, and the check of rows leading past the index left the refused numbers out; the stream-end search judged a header a later read found with what it had read past that header's start. Its fixes brought the stream-end search's and the object scan's benchmarks.
  - The second, 24 findings, three major: an `/XRefStm`'s search stopped at what the table's `/Prev` names where that `/Prev` keeps no place there — a font, a CIDFont that gives a `/W`, a string —, the stream beyond never read; a `/Prev` a few bytes short of a section the `/XRefStm` had read in the stream's place passed it over as reached, skipping a revision or rebuilding the file; and the word `xref` in a comment or a string ended a relocation.
  - The third, 18 findings, four major: whether that `/Prev` keeps its place was told from its dictionary alone, now by a check that reads the section as the chain would and keeps nothing of it; the line test lexed a place's line from the bytes searched, now from the line's start, read back up to 4,096 bytes, an internal constant; a stray header of `/Type /XRef` ended a relocation as the word had; and `xref.hybrid-stream-wrong` blamed the file where a guard's cut decided the place.
  - The fourth, 27 findings, one blocker and two majors: the line test threw, or read without end, on a source that serves short reads, and the decision on an `endstream` a comment holds failed on one too; and the look at a `/Prev`'s place read the object from its header where the `/Prev`'s own reading read from the offset named, so that near `MaxTrailerLength` the two disagreed, which the maintainer settled by reading every named section from its first token. The search for a classic table's trailer past rows a guard left unread, which reads a short window again without end, was found older than the batch and filed ([#331]).
  - The fifth, 18 findings, none major: what white space past `MaxTrailerLength` at an `/XRefStm`'s offset now leads to, left to [#289]; a section read in part reported before what it refused, its message then wrong; the threat model's gap on short reads, split in two.
  - The sixth, 14 findings, one major: the search near an offset takes `xref`, or a header of `/Type /XRef`, in uncompressed stream data, a section the file does not have, worse than `main`, documented and tracked under [#171]; and the threat model understated what short reads spend of the `endstream` searches' budget.
  - The seventh, 21 findings, two major, each worse than `main`: a cross-reference stream whose filters decoded nothing had its encoded bytes read as rows, zlib's first byte a reserved type since [#221], so that an object read as null; and a hybrid table's `/Prev` took the update's own stream, which the second round's fixes had tried last, the chain ending there, the file rebuilt and a freed object served.
  - The eighth, 32 findings, nine major, six once duplicates are merged: dropping a place whose data decodes to nothing took with it what reading its dictionary had reported, which nothing made again; a guard's cut of `/Size` or of the whole `/Index` hid such data, the guard blamed; a stream passed over was counted twice against the places the search may try, so that a section 5 bytes off was lost; a dictionary `MaxTrailerLength` cut decided whether a stream was the table's own, the guard unreported; and a first revision's stream, which gives no `/Prev`, tried last as possibly the table's own, and a stream an `/XRefStm` names again escaping that order, which the maintainer's answers of that day settled. Since its fixes, a stream whose `/Size` or whole `/Index` a guard cut has its data decoded to tell whether it holds rows, so that `MaxStreamLength`, `MaxFilterChainLength` or `MaxDecodedStreamLength` may be reached, and thrown under `ThrowOnLimit`, before the guard that cut the value; the look at a `/Prev`'s place did so for a stream of `/Type /XRef` too, until the ninth round's fixes.
  - The ninth, 31 findings, three major: `xref.stream-passed-over` was made at a stream the chain had reached and could not read, and where a guard had stopped the chain or cut what it reached.
  - The tenth, 22 findings, eight major, three once duplicates are merged: the look at a `/Prev`'s place dropped a guard its decoding reached, which no later reading reported; the diagnostics counted a guard met again as one the capacity left out, with room to spare, since `e157359` and `68a55fa`; and the warning was made at a stream the chain had read from inside its object number.
  - The eleventh, 24 findings, one major, worse than `main`: a stream of `/Type /XRef` that cannot be read, its header written `+14 0 obj`, was dropped by the search, the next section farther off taken and the revision's objects served from an older one, with nothing said: `2b26d18`'s line test, asked at the digit [#327]'s walk finds, where no token starts.
  - The twelfth, 25 findings, one major, from the eleventh's fix: a header the chain reached at its sign was taken again at its digit, the section reported twice, a false loop, and a rebuild serving a freed object. A shape its fixes left, a header written `++5 0` with a `%` on its line, still looped where `main` read the chain whole, and was fixed before the thirteenth round.
  - The thirteenth, 23 findings: its two majors were refuted, both [#332]'s and no worse than `main`, and so was one minor; the 13 minors and 7 nits left were texts and tests, which the final pass, `d26df72`, `cd17a9a` and `19a2296`, fixed, with a comment on [#332].
- **Done**, in [#{{PR: the pull request's number}}], 173 commits from `4c744a5` to `19a2296`{{SETTLE_COMMITS: and the commits the ADR 53 step's settling added, to the branch's last}}:
  - [#188] and [#281]: a section the chain names is there where its offset holds the `xref` keyword or a stream whose dictionary gives `/Type /XRef`; one there that cannot be read is reported where it is, `xref.section-unreadable`, and never looked for nearby. The search near an offset that holds no section passes over every place where a section the chain reached starts — told by where its first token starts, the white space before it crossed once a position, or, for a stream reached from a sign of its header, by where its digits start —, and takes the nearest place that reads as a section, or that declares itself one and cannot be read, which ends the search there, the section found moved and unreadable. A place declares itself only where its keyword or header is a token its line, lexed alone from its start, reads — read back up to 4,096 bytes, `LineStartSearchLength`, an internal constant —, a signed header asked at its first sign; an object that gives only a `/W` stays a guess. The word in stream data, or on a line inside a string an earlier line opened, is still taken, as [#171] records.
  - [#197]: the search near an offset, for an object or a section, reaches 512 bytes either side of it wherever it falls; a window that does not start the file reads one byte before its start, and a header or a section counts only where it starts inside it, so that the window's edge no longer cuts a header's number — [#171]'s first bullet, for that search.
  - [#261]: the search near a row, the rebuild's scan and the search near an offset read a header with comments between its tokens as a load does, a line that holds a `%` lexed from its start by the load's lexer, the walk linear and allocating nothing; the rule a damaged stream's `endstream` search stops at, and the parser's end of the containers an object left open, take such a header too, an `endstream` inside its comment waiting for what follows the line, decided alike however a source parts its reads. A header whose number carries a sign is [#327]'s.
  - [#208]: a cross-reference stream of `/Type /XRef` whose rows cannot be read — a width negative or no number, a fault of its numbering, a deferred `/Length` no `endstream` confirms, data its filters decode nothing of — hands its `/Prev` on: the older sections are read and judged, its dictionary never taken for the trailer. Data left at a filter the library does not know, or Flate data corrupt before its first byte, holds no rows: the filter pipeline says so, and the encoded bytes are no longer read as rows.
  - [#207] and [#210]: a section read only in part — a table the end of the file cuts after a row, a row that is no row ending a subsection, a stream that holds fewer rows than it declares — is kept and reported once, at the section, under `xref.section-unreadable`, its message saying which rows stand; a table cut before its first row cannot be read, the file ending before its trailer. No older section's row stands for a number such a section lost, nor for one a stream that cannot be read declares, nor for what a classic table an `/XRefStm` names that cannot be read lost, every number where what follows its fault cannot be known: the ranges are kept to 4,096, `MaxRefusedRanges`, an internal constant, every number refused past them, and the rebuild finds the objects when they are asked for. `trailer.root-recovered` is reported once a document.
  - [#222]: a table that holds no subsection reads as indexing nothing, `xref.section-empty`, a repair.
  - [#156]: every `/XRefStm` naming is recorded, a stream two tables name read once and counted for each; one that names no cross-reference stream of its revision — the table itself, another table, a table read in the stream's place, a section the chain read, what its `/Prev` names — is `xref.hybrid-stream-lost`, a warning, the index left incomplete, and is not followed where its `/Prev` names the same section. The search near a hybrid table's `/Prev` tells the table's own stream by where it lies — at or after the stream the `/XRefStm` read or names again — and by its `/Prev` as the chain reads it, one that gives none, or the table's, tried last; the stream the `/XRefStm` read is told by its header's digits, a place inside its object number or at a sign of it included; places tried are counted once each, 32 a document at most, a retry uncounted, the place a hybrid table's `/Prev` names still asked past them. A stream it passes over that declares itself, which the chain never reads, is reported, `xref.stream-passed-over`, a warning, but nowhere in a document where a limit stopped the chain or cut what it reached, a trailer guard cut the reading where the chain names an offset — but for the trailer of a classic table an `/XRefStm` names, whose links the chain never follows —, or white space there ran past `MaxTrailerLength`.
  - [#264]: what a section a rows guard stopped names — `MaxXRefSectionLength`, `MaxDecodedStreamLength`, `MaxIndexEntries` — is judged as a whole read judges it, nothing past the guard read (ADR 34, amended on 2026-11-04).
  - a section a link names is read from the first token past its white space, the white space and the dictionary each bounded by `MaxTrailerLength`, a classic table's white space counting toward `MaxXRefSectionLength` as well ([#289]); white space past it reaches `limit.trailer`, reported at the offset, the section not read from there; the probe reads a few bytes past the window, and crosses each position once.
  - [#221]: a cross-reference stream row of a reserved type reads as the null object, no older row standing for it, `xref.entry-reserved`, a warning, until a rebuild for another fault reads its object as the scan finds it.
  - the validator: `xref.subsection-missing` ([#222]) and `xref.entry-type-reserved` ([#221]), warnings, and `xref.hybrid-stream-wrong` ([#156]), an error; `file.size-wrong` counts a stream two tables name for each, judges its `/Size` where the oldest names it, and judges no chain that holds a section read in part or ends in a loop, so that the PDFMaker 7 document loses its false finding; `xref.section-malformed` covers a section read in part; `xref.chain-loop` keeps to `/Prev`; `xref.offset-imprecise` counts each offset written once; `file.trailer-value-wrong` judges a handed `/Prev`, and a hybrid stream's own `/Prev`, read only to tell its place, as written.
  - [#241]: a count agrees with its noun, and a distance says "before" or "after", through `CountText`, which the reader and the validator share; each site keeps its digit grouping ([#326]).
  - the diagnostics count apart the guards' reports their capacity drops, so that a rollback beside the guards counts left out only those.
  - [#317]: the test's probe takes a validation's default room for findings, 1,000, and times four probes back to back each turn under its bound of 8: under four busy loops, 300 of 300 runs of the classes together and 100 of 100 alone passed, at ratios of 2.1 to 7.7, one at 7.7 and the rest at most 6.6, and 3.7 to 4.6 over 50 runs without load; the scan of the header for each member, which the test guards against, was caught in 45 of 45 runs, at 13.1 to 21.9. The review's fourth round saw 9.07, 9.68 and 15.8 under a heavier load: the issue closes on four busy loops, not on a machine run past its cores. The timing of 1,024 chained streams that cannot be read is held under 12, 3.2 to 6.2 over 300 runs under four busy loops and up to 10.0 with builds beside them.
  - `StreamEndSearchBenchmarks` and `ObjectScanBenchmarks`; `HybridFiles`, `NearbyFiles`, `PartialFiles`, `LostStreamFiles`, `ReservedRowFiles`, `StoppedSectionFiles` and `UndecodedStreamFiles` building the files, and `CappedReadSource`, `CutShortSource` and `ShortCountSource` serving short reads; the tests that hide a header from the scan write its generation with a sign, through one helper.

  Docs: ADR 34, amended on 2026-11-04; ADR 46's spelling; the threat model's rows on the chain's search near an offset, the sections read in part and the numbers refused, the stream-end search and the scan, undecodable data and what a dropped place leaves, and its known gaps, [#156], [#188], [#197], [#221] and [#261]'s gone, [#52], [#171] and [#279]'s widened, [#47]'s restated, and [#320] to [#325] and [#327] to [#333] new; M02's specification of the chain and its corpus row; M04's revision of a stream several tables name; the row of `docs/corpus-contributions.md` for a real update whose middle section is damaged; `concepts/diagnostics.md`, `concepts/lazy-reading.md`, `concepts/reader-limits.md`, `concepts/validation.md`, `reference/diagnostics.md`, `reference/reader-limits.md` and `reference/validation-rules.md`.
- **Tests**: {{UNIT: the new unit tests and the whole unit suite on the committed corpus at the branch's last code, from 6,657 at `21a0298`, with passed and skipped}} — with the remote documents, from 9,817 at the implementation's end with the 235 the container held to 11,052 at `19a2296` with all 242, on the maintainer's machine, 11,049 passing and 3 skipped, {{UNIT_REMOTE: the same count at the branch's last code}} —, in `CrossReferenceChainTests`, `CrossReferenceRuleTests`, `GuardCutValueTests`, `ReaderLimitsTests`, `HostileInputTests`, `DocumentReaderTests`, `WindowEdgeTests`, `StreamLengthTests`, `PropertyTests`, `ReaderTimingTests`, `HeaderTextTests`, `DiagnosticsTests`, `FileRuleTests`, `CountTextTests`, `EndObjTailCopiesTests` and their neighbors; the integration suite unchanged, {{INTEGRATION: its count against qpdf at the branch's last code, passed and skipped}}.
- **Mutation**: ADR 53's step at each stage of the implementation and of the fixes that changed code in `src/`, then over the whole batch at its end. The review corrected the records of six stages: a mutant of the stage on undecodable data, one of the stage that read a stream's own `/Prev` through references and one of the stage on decoding beside a cut value, each classed equivalent, each killed once a row told it apart; three classed equivalent at the stage that told a stream by its data, whose reasons failed for a run of signs, classed again on the code that followed, and two mutants of its digit range it had left out, then killed, one reason of the stage before it corrected, a stream's first byte able to be a `+`; and three of the window-edge stage, killed by the final pass's rows of a section at the file's first byte and of longer runs of signs. Four survivors of the twelfth round's reached test went with the change that followed, three of their edges pinned by fourteen rows, the fourth [#332]'s. The step at the end took as its catalog the 1,746 code lines of `src/` the batch changes, 2,171 sites and 3,751 mutants, of which 15 a stage record still covered — its replacement, its killing test and the code it flows into unchanged at `19a2296` —, 388 of the 529 records read refused, each with its reason; the 3,736 others were run, each stopping at its first failing test, 224 of them expected not to compile and built to confirm it. {{ADR53: batch-wide tally — viable, killed, equivalent and equivalent in what is reported, survivors filed as debt, not viable — with the tests written to kill survivors and their commits}}. Two sites no test reaches, a defensive branch of the parse of an object no section records and the line test's guard against a place before the bytes it read, were expected to survive, beside the clamp of the probe's reach to an array's greatest length, which only a run past 2 GB tells apart: {{ADR53_DEBT: the issue that files the survivors, its number, title and milestone}}.
- **Tracking.** Filed under M02 on 2026-11-03, from the measurement: [#320], the rows a relocation candidate leaves in the index when it is dropped; [#321], an `/XRefStm` copied two revisions or more late; [#322], a table whose `trailer` keyword no dictionary follows, ending the chain in silence; [#323], a first section that cannot be read reported only as a rebuild; [#324], a classic table mid-chain whose bad row, subsection header or stray token ends the chain; [#325], `xref.section-malformed` silent where the trailer is at fault too; [#326], counts grouped in some messages and not others, and one fault of an object stream's header worded two ways; [#327], a header whose number or generation carries a sign, missed or misread by the scan and the searches; [#328], a `/Prev` in a trailer `MaxTrailerLength` cut, never judged. On 2026-11-04, after the first round: [#329], the `/Prev` of a stream at which a value's cut ends the chain, never judged; [#330], the mirror order of [#156]. On 2026-11-06, under M03: [#331], the search for a classic table's trailer that a source serving short reads sends round one window without end, older than the batch. On 2026-11-08: [#332], a `/Prev` a few bytes off a stream a newer table's `/XRefStm` read, which never takes it, blocked by [#330]; [#333], an older revision's stream nearer a hybrid table's `/Prev` than its own table taken, what only the sections it skips index lost. On 2026-11-10: [#336], the test that every table keyed by numbers takes the seeded comparer, which fails under the coverage command on Windows, reading IL the profiler rewrote, older than the batch. Commented: [#171], seven times — the left edge paid, two more ways a header is read otherwise than a load reads it, the chain's search near an offset as a fourth such reader, the word or a stream's header in stream data, edited after the seventh round, a section there that reads, a quoted stream passed over, and a table whose keyword a comment holds, taken under a guard or read whole, edited on 2026-11-10 —; [#289], four times, the warning the white-space guard withholds, its reach throughout the document, a comment between a header's tokens counted toward `MaxTrailerLength`, and any cut withholding it; [#327], four times, the search near an offset asking a signed header at its first sign, its reached test doing so too, the two edges that left fixed, and the `%` line's loop fixed; [#332], four times, each check that tells a stream the chain read by a byte, and its body's first item corrected; [#279], three times, a `/Type` value a cut parts, any dictionary cut, then any cut; [#260], twice, two more ways to `file.root-invalid`'s false sentence, a trailer with no `/Root` whose catalog a rebuild finds in an older one, and a file whose only section gives only reserved rows; [#320], twice, what the first round's decision changes of its shape, and the refusals and the flag a dropped candidate leaves too; [#330], twice, its shape with the `/Prev` a few bytes short of the stream and one byte into its number; [#322], the converse of its shape, rows that run into `<<` with no `trailer`; [#292], the same rebuild through a rows guard and `MaxObjectLength`, which its fix must cover; [#52], the two readers the batch added that a source serving short reads broke, and how they are guarded; [#336]'s body, a line number corrected. {{END_COMMENTS: the comments posted at the batch's end — #289, what the batch changed of a guard reached in white space and its closing, with the probe's costs; #295, the cost measured through a long run before a table; #52, the shape of two streams that spend the endstream searches' budget; #260 if posted}}. The pull request closes [#156], [#188], [#197], [#207], [#208], [#210], [#221], [#222], [#241], [#261], [#264], [#281] and [#317].
- **Checked**, at {{HEAD: the branch's last commit, after the ADR 53 step's tests}} unless said:
  - the unit suite under code coverage, as CI runs it, on the committed corpus: {{COVERAGE: tests, passed and skipped; of the patch's coverable lines in `src/`, those run and those whole, and its branches, each line not whole with its reason}}; at `dfb03fc`, of the patch's 1,462 coverable lines 1,460 run and 1,456 whole, the six not whole a defensive branch of the parse of an object no section records (two lines), the line test's guard against a place before the bytes it read (two), a branch the compiler adds in `SizeWrongRule` (71) and the defensive arm of the walk past a header's signs, none of which an input takes. On Windows the coverage command fails [#336]'s test, older than the batch, and once failed a timing row under load, both passing alone;
  - with all 242 remote documents, on the maintainer's machine, 11,052 unit tests at `19a2296`, 11,049 passed and 3 skipped; the timing, budget, allocation, memory and hostile-input groups three times each, alone, at every round's check from the seventh, all passing; the integration suite against qpdf: {{INTEGRATION: count, passed and skipped, and the commits it ran on}};
  - the corpus opened and validated on `main` and on the branch at every round's check — 403 documents in the container until the eighth, 410 on the maintainer's machine from the ninth, 168 committed and 242 remote —: once [#241]'s 1,072 rewordings in 28 documents are applied to `main`'s reports, one document differs, the PDFMaker 7 document of the OPF corpus, which loses its false `file.size-wrong`; no document threw; {{CORPUS: the same at the branch's last code}};
  - the 9,228 readings of the generated hybrid files, the same at every round's check from the eighth but where a decision changed them;
  - the package in the compatibility island's trimmed application on .NET 11: {{ISLAND: documents opened and validated, refused, differing, and the commit}};
  - the documentation site, 269 pages, no warning, at `19a2296`; formatting clean; every commit message checked against the project's rules;
  - {{BENCH: the benchmarks run — ReaderBenchmarks, IndexBenchmarks, ValidationBenchmarks, CrossReferenceProbeBenchmarks, StreamLengthBenchmarks, TrailerScanBenchmarks, StreamEndSearchBenchmarks and ObjectScanBenchmarks —, how, and against `main`}}: *Current measurements* gives the figures;
